# Songhealth production PostgreSQL container (Feature 001, T019).
#
# Authority:
# - specs/001-platform-foundation/tasks.md T019 — the pinned PostgreSQL 18.6
#   rootless Quadlet with private-network-only attachment, the persistent
#   database volume, external secrets/configuration, native container health,
#   and no published host port
# - docs/website-modernization-docs/current/10-system-blueprint.md §2 (pin
#   image versions by immutable digest in production definitions), §3.1
#   (rootless Podman + systemd Quadlet baseline: PostgreSQL stays on the
#   private application network and is never host-published; secrets and
#   environment-specific configuration are never baked into images or
#   committed), §20/§20.1 (environment-specific values stay external under
#   supported mechanisms; the native PG* runtime contract's identity
#   components — host, port, database, user — are non-secret)
# - specs/001-platform-foundation/plan.md — "Production topology and
#   durability" (one PostgreSQL .container on the private network with the
#   database volume mounted; user systemd owns start/stop/restart/ordering/
#   restart policy; no PostgreSQL port is published), "Configuration and
#   secrets" (non-secret values may use an external environment file;
#   passwords stay in operator-managed Podman/systemd secrets), and the
#   approved role model: the application uses one stable, non-superuser,
#   least-privilege role (database connect, schema usage/create, DML, and
#   ownership of the EmDash objects it creates)
# - official postgres:18 image contract — the image initializes POSTGRES_USER
#   as the cluster bootstrap administrator WITH SUPERUSER privileges and
#   creates POSTGRES_DB owned by it, so this unit treats POSTGRES_USER and
#   the songhealth-db-password secret as the bootstrap administrator's
#   identity, never as the application's role or credential
# - scripts/dev-db.sh — the approved PostgreSQL 18.6 image pin and the
#   storage-boundary research: official PostgreSQL 18 images declare
#   VOLUME /var/lib/postgresql with PGDATA=/var/lib/postgresql/18/docker, so
#   the persistent volume must mount the PARENT directory; the pre-18
#   /var/lib/postgresql/data boundary would leave the real data directory
#   unpersisted
# - deploy/quadlet/songhealth.network and songhealth-db-data.volume (T018),
#   referenced below by their Quadlet unit names so the generated user-systemd
#   service carries native ordering/dependency relationships on those
#   resources; tests/operations/verify-foundation.sh (T016) resolves the
#   container as systemd-songhealth-db, so ContainerName is deliberately NOT
#   set and keeps the Quadlet default naming (systemd-<unit-base>)
#
# No host port is published in any form — no PublishPort, ExposeHostPort, or
# any other host-port binding exists here, not even on loopback. The
# application reaches this database only over the private songhealth network;
# T016's C3 stage proves the boundary from runtime metadata and from the host
# side at deployment verification time.
#
# External runtime inputs (never committed; every absence fails closed):
#
# - ~/.config/containers/systemd/songhealth-db.env — the operator-managed,
#   non-secret bootstrap identity, loaded at the [Service] level (the %h
#   specifier is expanded by user systemd; no host-specific absolute path is
#   committed):
#     POSTGRES_USER=<bootstrap administrator name — an administrative
#                    identity, deliberately NOT the application's PGUSER>
#     POSTGRES_DB=<target application database>
#   A missing file fails the unit before any container exists; a missing or
#   empty required key skips the unit before the container is created (see
#   the ExecCondition gates in [Service]), so the image's fallback defaults
#   (superuser `postgres`, database named after the user) can never silently
#   initialize persistent storage. Only these two named variables are
#   forwarded into the container; no other key in the file reaches it.
#
# - the Podman secret `songhealth-db-password` — the bootstrap
#   administrator's credential, injected through the native Quadlet Secret=
#   mechanism and mounted at /run/secrets/songhealth-db-password (default
#   mode 0444). It is consumed through the official image's native
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE input, so no password value is embedded in this
#   file, in the container environment, or in Git, and `podman inspect`
#   cannot leak it. Container creation fails when the secret does not exist,
#   and the image itself additionally refuses to initialize without a
#   password.
#
# Application-role provisioning prerequisite (external; NOT a startup input
# of this unit): the application must never connect as the bootstrap
# administrator. Before application use, the operator provisions — from
# external deployment configuration, against this running database — the one
# stable, least-privilege application role matching the application runtime's
# PGUSER, with its own separately protected credential (a distinct operator
# secret consumed by the application unit, never songhealth-db-password) and
# exactly the approved permissions: database CONNECT, schema USAGE/CREATE,
# DML, and ownership of the EmDash objects it creates — no superuser,
# CREATEDB, or CREATEROLE. Provisioning cannot precede first initialization,
# so it does not gate this unit's start; application credentials and
# migrations are enforced by the application-side startup/readiness gate
# (T013/T020). The provisioned role is verifiable secret-free through the
# local administrative boundary (its pg_roles attributes must show
# non-superuser and its database/schema privileges must match the approved
# set); provisioning commands and their evidence remain disposable operator
# material and are not committed to this repository.
#
# Existing-database-store guard (fail-closed, bounded source correction):
# plan.md forbids that "a missing expected attachment or an accidentally
# empty replacement store ... appear[s] as a healthy platform", but the
# official image's native entrypoint silently runs initdb against ANY empty
# PGDATA — including a named volume that Podman recreated automatically
# after the original persistent volume disappeared (the T018 volume unit
# generates `podman volume create --ignore`, and any `-v name:...` mount
# auto-creates a missing named volume as an empty replacement). Without
# this guard, a deleted-and-recreated songhealth-db-data volume would come
# up as a fresh empty cluster and report healthy. The gate below therefore
# refuses to start unless the persistent store ALREADY holds an initialized
# PostgreSQL cluster or carries the operator's one-time provisioning
# marker. Its exact contract and honest limits:
#
# - What is checked: a non-empty PG_VERSION at
#   /var/lib/postgresql/18/docker — the exact marker the official image's
#   own entrypoint consults (same digest, PGDATA at that path per the
#   pinned-image research above) to decide "PostgreSQL Database directory
#   appears to contain a database; Skipping initialization" — OR an
#   operator provisioning marker file
#   .songhealth-db-store at the volume root /var/lib/postgresql (the same
#   store-marker pattern as the reviewed T020 media-store prerequisite;
#   deliberately OUTSIDE PGDATA so the native entrypoint never touches
#   it). Named-volume existence alone proves nothing and is never
#   consulted.
#
# - Where it runs, and the three explicitly distinct claims: the guard is
#   a [Service] ExecCondition, evaluated after the T018 volume unit (the
#   generated Requires=/After= on songhealth-db-data-volume.service) but
#   strictly BEFORE the database container is created. It does NOT prevent
#   the T018 volume unit (or Podman itself) from creating a missing named
#   volume as an empty replacement — preventing that creation would
#   require changing T018 and is outside this unit's authority; the honest
#   claim is the pair it does establish: it DOES prevent PostgreSQL from
#   initializing that replacement store (on refusal the unit is skipped —
#   exit 1-254 semantics: not failed, not restarted — so no container
#   exists, the native entrypoint never runs, initdb never executes
#   against the unvalidated store), and it DOES prevent false Healthy/
#   Ready acceptance (the unit never reaches active, Notify=healthy can
#   never fire, and the T020 application unit's Requires=songhealth-db.
#   service therefore also refuses to start — no application ever connects
#   to a refused store).
#
# - The probes: one-shot `podman run --rm --network=none` invocations of
#   the SAME pinned digest below, mounting songhealth-db-data READ-ONLY at
#   /var/lib/postgresql and running only /usr/bin/test through the image's
#   native entrypoint command passthrough (a non-postgres first argument is
#   exec'd as-is by docker-entrypoint.sh). They read two file-existence
#   facts, create nothing, mutate nothing, publish no port, attach to no
#   network, and remove themselves (--rm). They are read-only validation
#   probes of the one approved database image, not a second PostgreSQL
#   container and not an orchestration layer.
#
# - Intentional first-time provisioning is explicit and operator-controlled
#   (this unit, this image, and every committed file arm nothing by
#   default):
#     1. podman volume create songhealth-db-data
#        (idempotent; only needed if the volume does not exist yet — the
#        T018 unit would create it empty at first start anyway)
#     2. arm provisioning ONCE by deliberately placing the marker inside
#        the persistent store itself, with the same pinned image:
#          podman run --rm \
#            --volume=songhealth-db-data:/var/lib/postgresql:rw \
#            docker.io/library/postgres:18.6@sha256:fc973eb97c9fd04bfa1840e0f510719a584ccb3be8debfe6a4144637a9dfe8cf \
#            touch /var/lib/postgresql/.songhealth-db-store
#     3. systemctl --user daemon-reload (if this unit changed) and
#        systemctl --user start songhealth-db.service: the gate passes on
#        the marker, the journal records that provisioning mode is active,
#        and the native entrypoint performs initdb exactly as the image
#        defines it (bootstrap POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_DB identity + the
#        songhealth-db-password secret; no second initialization path
#        exists).
#     4. verify healthy (systemctl --user is-active songhealth-db.service
#        and the unit journal), provision the least-privilege application
#        role per the prerequisite above, then REMOVE the marker:
#          podman run --rm \
#            --volume=songhealth-db-data:/var/lib/postgresql:rw \
#            docker.io/library/postgres:18.6@sha256:fc973eb97c9fd04bfa1840e0f510719a584ccb3be8debfe6a4144637a9dfe8cf \
#            rm -f /var/lib/postgresql/.songhealth-db-store
#   The marker lives INSIDE the persistent bytes, so an automatically
#   recreated replacement volume NEVER inherits it: re-arming
#   initialization always requires the operator's explicit marker command
#   against that specific new store. No permanently enabled bootstrap
#   bypass exists in this unit, the image, or any committed configuration;
#   daily operation never depends on the marker. Leaving it in place after
#   provisioning is a documented operator error with bounded effect: it
#   would re-arm initialization only for an in-place wiped PGDATA within
#   that same marked volume — never for a replaced volume — and the
#   provisioning-mode journal notice reports its presence on every start
#   that passes through it.
#
# - Existing stores and normal lifecycle are unchanged: any store with an
#   initialized cluster passes on the PG_VERSION arm alone (marker present
#   or absent), so ordinary stop/start/restart/container-recreation
#   behavior, the health contract, and the restart policy behave exactly
#   as before. An existing volume whose cluster state is missing or
#   invalid fails closed: an absent or empty PG_VERSION is refused by this
#   gate (unless provisioning was deliberately armed); a present-but-
#   corrupt cluster passes the marker check and is then refused by
#   PostgreSQL itself (native startup failure, never healthy, restart
#   policy with systemd's start rate limiting bounds the retry). Nothing
#   is ever created, repaired, formatted, deleted, or reformatted
#   automatically when validation fails.

[Unit]
Description=Songhealth PostgreSQL 18.6 database (private application network only)

[Container]
# Official PostgreSQL 18.6 image, pinned by explicit version and the immutable
# digest of the upstream multi-arch index — the same approved pin
# scripts/dev-db.sh uses (verified 2026-10-07 against registry metadata).
Image=docker.io/library/postgres:18.6@sha256:fc973eb97c9fd04bfa1840e0f510719a584ccb3be8debfe6a4144637a9dfe8cf

# Attach exclusively to the accepted T018 private application network. The
# Quadlet reference generates Requires=/After= on songhealth-network.service
# and attaches the container to the network that unit defines (NetworkName=
# songhealth).
Network=songhealth.network

# Persistent database data: the accepted T018 volume (VolumeName=songhealth
# -db-data) mounted at the PostgreSQL 18 persistent parent of PGDATA, per the
# dev-db.sh storage-boundary research above. The Quadlet reference generates
# a dependency on songhealth-db-data-volume.service; container recreation
# replaces only the ephemeral container layer, never the named volume.
Volume=songhealth-db-data.volume:/var/lib/postgresql

# Bootstrap identity: the two [Service]-validated variables are forwarded
# into the container by bare name — podman --env KEY inherits each value from
# the unit environment that user systemd loaded from the external environment
# file. The image's native entrypoint and initialization behavior are
# unchanged, and no other key from that file reaches the container.
Environment=POSTGRES_USER
Environment=POSTGRES_DB

# Bootstrap credential: protected external Podman secret, mounted as a file at
# the default /run/secrets/songhealth-db-password and consumed through the
# official image's native *_FILE input. No usable credential is committed or
# exported into the environment; a missing secret fails container creation.
Secret=songhealth-db-password
Environment=POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/songhealth-db-password

# Native container health, in the same accepted shape as scripts/dev-db.sh:
# the official image's pg_isready against the container's own non-secret
# identity inputs, on the container-internal loopback. No secret is involved
# or printed. Notify=healthy postpones the generated unit's startup
# notification until the health check passes, so user systemd never considers
# the service started before PostgreSQL is healthy. pg_isready proves only
# that the server accepts connections; the ExecCondition gates in [Service]
# are what prevent wrong-identity initialization and the initialization of
# an unprovisioned, empty, or replacement store.
HealthCmd=pg_isready -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d "$POSTGRES_DB" -h 127.0.0.1
HealthInterval=5s
HealthTimeout=3s
HealthRetries=3
HealthStartPeriod=30s
Notify=healthy

# Rootless user-systemd lifecycle behavior — no alternative orchestration
# layer exists, and no boot-time enablement is defined here: the unit runs
# when the operator starts or enables it through user systemd.
#
# The environment file supplies the bootstrap identity at the systemd level.
# A missing file fails the unit load before any container exists, and the
# ExecCondition gates skip the unit — exit 1-254 semantics: skipped, not
# failed, and not restarted — when a required identity value is missing or
# empty, or when the existing-database-store guard below refuses the
# persistent store. In every gated case no container is created, the native
# image entrypoint never runs, the persistent volume is never initialized
# with fallback defaults or as a replacement store, and the healthy
# notification cannot fire. Failures name the field or the failed store
# condition only; values are never printed.
#
# Existing-database-store guard (see the header section above for the full
# contract): one shell line whose probes run BEFORE the database container
# is created — first the initialized-cluster arm (non-empty PG_VERSION at
# the pinned image's PGDATA, the same marker the native entrypoint
# consults), then, only if the store is not initialized, the operator's
# one-time provisioning marker arm (which also announces provisioning mode
# in the journal), and otherwise a refusal that names the failed condition
# and skips the unit. Both probes are one-shot `podman run --rm
# --network=none` invocations of the same pinned digest mounting
# songhealth-db-data READ-ONLY at /var/lib/postgresql and running only
# /usr/bin/test; they read two file-existence facts, write nothing, and
# create nothing. All command paths are absolute; no environment variable
# of this unit is expanded into the line, and the diagnostics name only
# volumes, paths, and conditions — never credentials, values, or database
# contents.
[Service]
EnvironmentFile=%h/.config/containers/systemd/songhealth-db.env
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${POSTGRES_USER}"
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${POSTGRES_DB}"
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/sh -c 'podman run --rm --network=none --volume=songhealth-db-data:/var/lib/postgresql:ro docker.io/library/postgres:18.6@sha256:fc973eb97c9fd04bfa1840e0f510719a584ccb3be8debfe6a4144637a9dfe8cf /usr/bin/test -s /var/lib/postgresql/18/docker/PG_VERSION || { podman run --rm --network=none --volume=songhealth-db-data:/var/lib/postgresql:ro docker.io/library/postgres:18.6@sha256:fc973eb97c9fd04bfa1840e0f510719a584ccb3be8debfe6a4144637a9dfe8cf /usr/bin/test -e /var/lib/postgresql/.songhealth-db-store && /usr/bin/echo "songhealth-db: provisioning marker .songhealth-db-store is present in songhealth-db-data: initial cluster initialization is enabled for this start - remove the marker after first-time provisioning is verified healthy" >&2; } || { /usr/bin/echo "songhealth-db: refusing to start: the persistent store (named volume songhealth-db-data at /var/lib/postgresql) holds no initialized PostgreSQL cluster (no non-empty PG_VERSION at /var/lib/postgresql/18/docker) and no operator provisioning marker (.songhealth-db-store) - an empty, missing-content, or automatically recreated replacement store is never initialized silently. Intentional first-time provisioning: create the marker, start this unit, verify healthy, then remove the marker - see the existing-database-store guard section in this unit header" >&2; exit 1; }'

# The unit returns after any unplanned container exit (postgres crash, OOM
# kill, external signal shutdown); an explicit `systemctl --user stop` is
# never restarted, and systemd's built-in start rate limiting bounds the
# retry loop of a fail-closed missing input.
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s
